            <ArticleTitle>Longitudinal trajectories of perceived body weight: adolescence to early adulthood.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>242-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5993/AJHB.36.2.9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine longitudinal trajectories of perceived weight from adolescence to early adulthood by gender.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We analyzed 9 waves (1997-2005) of data from the National Longitudinal Survey of Youth (N = 8302) using Mplus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Perceived overweight increased over time among girls and did not level off until 23 years of age. Blacks had a lower perceived weight for their actual weight and a slower rate of increase in perceived weight than did whites.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Intervention programs designed to prevent or reduce obesity should evaluate weight perceptions for both adolescents and young adults prior to implementing each intervention.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
